### Table 3 (2) CL Councillors - Water quality: Medium
  published -0.393 | ours -3.891
  diagnosis: Standard error printed in the coefficient cell (with a minus sign)
  evidence:  our standard error is 0.3926, which rounds to |-0.393|; every other model-2 number matches
  verdict:   Explained: reporting error in the paper

### Table 3 (2) CL Councillors - Water quality: Low
  published -0.602 | ours -6.184
  diagnosis: Standard error printed in the coefficient cell (with a minus sign)
  evidence:  our standard error is 0.6017, which rounds to |-0.602|; every other model-2 number matches
  verdict:   Explained: reporting error in the paper

### Table 3 (1) CL All - Residential x $ shown
  published 0.670 (p 0.018) | ours 0.699 (p 0.018)
  diagnosis: Coefficient mistyped; the printed p-value is the one our coefficient produces
  evidence:  with SE 0.295, 0.670 would give p = 0.023, but the paper prints 0.018 = p for 0.699. No alternative sample or coding we tried gives 0.670
  verdict:   Explained: typo in the paper (0.699 is correct)

### Table 3 (1) CL All - Stables & landscape (p-value)
  published 0.031 (**) | ours 0.001 (***)
  diagnosis: p-value (and its **) likely left over from an earlier run on all 180 respondents
  evidence:  the same model on all 180 respondents gives p = 0.031 for this row; Table 1 also uses all 180. The coefficient in that run (-0.985) differs, so the match is suggestive, not proof
  verdict:   Likely explained: stale value from a different sample

### Table 1 - City inhabitants (4 rows)
  published 36.7 / 30.9 / 21.3 / 11.2 | ours 39.1 / 29.0 / 20.1 / 11.8
  diagnosis: Computed on a different (larger) version of the data than the one published
  evidence:  the published shares are whole-number counts only for n = [188, 376] (n=188: 69/58/40/21); the file has 180 respondents, 169 of whom answered (66/49/34/20). Other samples don't match either: {'finished': [39.3, 29.4, 19.0, 12.3], 'complete 164': [40.4, 28.2, 19.2, 12.2]}. The gender, age and residence rows fit the published file exactly
  verdict:   Explained as far as the public data allow: not reproducible from S1 Table

### Table 3 (4) ML Councillors - Residential x $ shown (stars)
  published 3.450** (p 0.062) | ours n/a (simulation-based column)
  diagnosis: Significance stars inconsistent with the printed p-value
  evidence:  ** means p < 0.05 in the table's own legend, but the printed p is 0.062 (would be *)
  verdict:   Explained: reporting error in the paper

